+* Burst test: many connections open at the same moment, all for the same lead (the same email under different request numbers). To repeat a small one, start the demo (README, "Try it") and run `python -m demo_only.burst --url http://127.0.0.1:8765/leads --n 100 --email ada@example.com --ref-prefix run1 --source form` (with `PYTHONPATH=src:.`). The address must be on this computer (`127.0.0.1`, `localhost`, `::1`) and use `http`: any other address ends at once with exit code 2 and one line, before any name lookup or connection. The script prints `SENT`, `STATUS` (for one lead sent many times expect `201=1` and `200=` all the others), `RETRIES` and then one last line: `RESULT sent=<n> expected=<n>` and exit code 0 when the two numbers are equal, or `MISMATCH sent=<n> expected=<n>` and exit code 1 when they differ; it does not print the lead count. Read the counts from `http://127.0.0.1:8765/counts` (or the portal Overview): for one lead sent many times, the lead count and the follow-up count should each be 1 (`tests/test_http.py::test_ten_parallel` checks this for 10). Right after a burst a follow-up can show as pending for a short time before the count of pending follow-ups returns to 0 (no length of time was measured here).
 * Larger bursts: one measurement test is shipped, `LEAD_DESK_LOAD_N=300 python -m pytest tests/test_server_hardening.py -k measure_burst -s -p no:cacheprovider` (add `LEAD_DESK_LOAD_RETRY=1` to let the sender retry a refused connection). It prints how many connections the operating system refused and the status counts. The waiting line is 1024 connections (`LISTEN_QUEUE` in `src/lead_desk/constants.py`) and the operating system can allow fewer. No result of this test is stored here, and no figure from it is claimed for any computer.
   * A connection that the operating system refuses is never seen by the desk, so the desk records nothing about it.
